# Bracha 1987, "Asynchronous Byzantine Agreement Protocols"
# Information and Computation 75, 130-143 (1987)
#
# Figure 3 -- the round with Broadcast and Validate, by process p.
#
#   round(k) by process p
#     Broadcast(p, k, v)
#     Wait till a set S of n - t k-messages have been validated
#     v := N(k, S)
#
# and VALID (Section 3): a k-message (q, k, v) is in VALID^k_p iff it
# is accepted, and for k = 1 v is 0 or 1, for k > 1 there exist n - t
# messages in VALID^(k-1)_p whose N is v.  "The processes update their
# VALID sets whenever they accept a message"; a message not validated
# is "still stored for future validation".
#
# From one process's seat: one Fig 1 instance per (q, k), carrying two
# facts beyond Fig 1's, accepted (Fig 1's output) and validated; per
# round, the count of validated k-messages.  One run of the tables per
# event on the instance the event belongs to; the Broadcast is that
# instance's Fig 1, its rows in bracha87Fig1.dtc, read here by name:
# "accepted" is Fig 1's, so a lone compile of this file takes it as an
# input, and the seat's C sets it from Figure 1's accept.
#
# A validation in round k - 1 can give a stored k-message its witness.
# The caller then runs these tables again on that stored instance; the
# decision at each visit is the validate row, nothing else.
#
# Inputs:
#   "accepted"                          { yes, no }  Fig 1's, this instance
#   "have validated"                    { yes, no }  this instance
#   "round of this message"             { 1, > 1 }
#   "value is 0 or 1"                   { yes, no }  read at round 1
#   "n-t messages in VALID^(k-1) yield v" { yes, no }  the witness; opaque
#                                       here, N's rows elsewhere
#   "validated messages of this round"  { < n-t, >= n-t, n }
#                                       this process's current round
#   "sweeps in this round"              { < patience, >= patience }
#                                       this process's completed sweeps
#                                       since it began its current round
#
# Outputs:
#   "validate (q, k, v)"                { yes, no }
#   "turn"                              { yes, no }  the round's wait is
#                                       over: v := N(k, S), Broadcast(p,
#                                       k+1, v), both the caller's

# ===== VALID =====

# A k-message is validated once: accepted, and at round 1 a value of 0
# or 1, above round 1 a witness among the validated (k-1)-messages.
@"validate (q, k, v)","accepted","have validated","round of this message","value is 0 or 1","n-t messages in VALID^(k-1) yield v"
yes,yes,no,1,yes,
no,yes,no,1,no,
yes,yes,no,> 1,,yes
no,yes,no,> 1,,no
no,yes,yes,,,
no,no,,,,

# ===== the round's wait =====

# "Wait till a set S of n - t k-messages have been validated."  The
# set keeps growing past n - t; the turn is taken at all n, or at
# n - t once the patience sweeps of this round have elapsed -- do not
# spend the Byzantine budget against a laggard.
@"turn","validated messages of this round","sweeps in this round"
yes,n,
yes,>= n-t,>= patience
no,>= n-t,< patience
no,< n-t,
